ui2/frontend/src/auth/LoginScreen.tsx reduces the response to an enum and throws the body away:

    if (response.ok) return "ok";
    if (response.status === 409) return "conflict";
    if (response.status === 401) return "invalid";
    return "unexpected";

and then:

        case "conflict":
          setError(CONFLICT_MESSAGE);
          return;

where `const CONFLICT_MESSAGE = "Another session is already active for this account.";`

The 409 body it discards is built by LoginFlow.conflictBody and carries exactly:

  error            the literal "LOGIN_CONFLICT"
  conflict_token   the token that authorizes the resolve call
  prior_session    an object with created_at and last_seen_at, both ISO instants

The resolve route is POST /login/resolve. Its request record is `record ResolveRequest(String conflictToken, String action)` and its action is the literal string "takeover" or anything else, which is treated as refuse. On takeover it answers 200 with `{"ok": true}` and sets the session cookie itself. On refusal it answers 409 with `error` LOGIN_REFUSED_ACTIVE_SESSION and a `reason_code`; the reason code for an expired or unrecognized token is `conflict_token_expired_or_unknown`. The route is deliberately not gated by E1-E6: the conflict token, not a session cookie, authorizes it.
